    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nbase16.lua tabline.lua\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nWhen using base16.lua and tabline.lua together, distinguishing between the active and modified tabs becomes quite perplexing due to their very similar contrast. What more is the modified state have a slightly higher contrast than the active one, adding to the confusion.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Neovim version\r\n\r\nNVIM v0.9.1\r\n\r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\n1. Enable base16.lua and tabline.lua.\r\n2. Open multiple buffers.\r\n3. Modify one buffer.\r\n3. Switch to a different buffer.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nMore distinction between active and modified state, lower contrast and perhaps a  icon? \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\n![image](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/91024200/4062a930-5977-4753-b635-d3998ced1ea1)\r\n![image](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/91024200/22d8c783-f32a-4f28-b594-844c0e92800f)\r\n![image](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/91024200/2bbf2584-6cd2-4d30-8ed6-135e8b2fe394)\r\n![image](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/assets/91024200/58af5898-a30a-4b2e-bd84-119c3f502618)\r\n\r\n\r\n",
